When a user edit last changed the value of an input or a textarea,
Ladybird reports tooShort and tooLong for it; when a script did (the
value setter, or setRangeText()), it reports neither. Blink keeps that
in TextControlElement::LastChangeWasUserEdit(), which HTMLInputElement::
TooShort() checks. WebKit keeps it in HTMLTextFormControlElement::
lastChangeWasUserEdit(), and Gecko in mLastValueChangeWasInteractive,
which its HTMLTextAreaElement::IsTooShort() and IsTooLong() check. Run
headless (Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5, with execCommand()’s insertText
standing in for typing), both report tooShort for a short edited value,
tooLong for an over-long value after a user deletion, and neither
after setRangeText(), as Ladybird does. Chrome 158 doesn’t count an
execCommand() edit as a user edit, so headless runs couldn’t show
what it does for typed text.
